plits  off at two  and any uh commits that we add onto that  and then the final problem i'm gonna  solve in in this little mini version  control system is being able to combine  code from different branches  so let's say we've been working on our  uh big feature for a while and we want  to bring it in from dev to main  so this is like a merge right  um let's say in our system emerge is  just another commit we don't actually  differentiate it at all  um but the only difference is we have  the history of all of the changes from  that branch so we need some way to  identify and resolve any conflicts that  happen between those two  uh code sets  so what i'm going to suggest is that we  compare files using the sha1 hash  and  what a hash function is if you're not  familiar is it's a function that takes  in  the variable length like a file or you  know some code whatever and it produces  a fixed length uh character string and  if it takes in exactly the same text  it'll produce the same hash  so we know for sure  if  we get the same hash of for two  different files those files are the same  and this is really helpful because it  means we can just  um you know accept any files where the  hashes match and then we can just flag  to the user any files that have  different hashes  so we'll just tell the user hey here's  the files that have some mismatch you  need to resolve the conflicts between  them  and just one thing i want to highlight  here is that this idea of a merge is  directional so when you're merging a  into b  um you're adding a commit onto branch b  right so that's a different commit  history than if you're adding uh b into  a  and just to show what that looks like in  our little tree structure so let's say  we want to pull  everything from that dev branch into  main  um  in that case we're just adding a commit  uh commit number seven onto maine this  is what it looks like in our history the  user maybe has to go through the effort  of figuring out how to resolve some  conflicts but  as far as what we're storing in our  system it's just another  commit great so now we have built a  really you know simple basic um  version control system let's see how git  does it  so um a confession for anyone who read  my abstract i mentioned something about  hand-drawn animation i actually um when  i wrote my proposal  um was under the impression that git  stores the changes between  uh commits in each like commit that it  makes and so i had this whole like um  analogy planned of like these animation  cells that you layer on top of each  other i thought it was really clever uh  but when i actually did the research for  the talk i realized this is the way that  a lot of version control systems work  but it's not how git works and git is  actually a lot simpler than this  um so  uh sorry if i misled you into into  coming to this talk but um you know i  learned a lot about git writing it so  that's very exciting for me  um so it actually turns out that git  does what we did which is kind of wild  to me it stores every file every time  you commit like the whole  repo essentially  so that means that every committed  version of every file is stored in the  dot get folder and the dot get folder is  just the equivalent of our vc folder it  stores the repository history for rega  so every file in its entirety is stored  there uh which seems wildly inefficient  at first glance but  you know it works really well actually  the the simplicity of it it's pretty  powerful  um so  let's talk about how git does  checkpointing so um obviously these are  uh commits  so um what git does when you run git  commit is it compresses  and using zlib which is an open source  compression library  and hashes using the sha-1 hash  every file in the repository  and then um the folder structure of your  repository is encoded in a bunch of tree  objects and a tree is essentially a  manifest so it lists out all of the  files and folders  um that are in the repo  and uh basically as like uh pointers to  the hashes  and those tree objects are just in files  it compresses them and it hashes them  and then it makes a commit object and  that's a file that lists the hash of the  tree and the hash of the parent commit  and then along with some user info and a  commit message  and then that's just a file right so it  compresses that and hashes that and  those all go  in the same place  so everything lives in this dot get  folder and then there's a subfolder  called objects so files commits trees  all of that compressed hash  lives in that folder all together  and every object this is actually really  clever really simple and clever is named  after its hash so that means that no  objects are duplicated because when you  make a commit if you haven't changed a  file it's going to have the same hash  so that means that file  uh that file name already exists it'll  just like overwrite it  and just a side note sometimes people  worry about like what if you hash two  different files and you get the same  hash this is called a hash collision  these are basically impossible um this  is so unlikely to happen and it's just  like not worth worrying about at all  um so let's actually look at some of  these objects so what's in a commit so  you can see this first line here uh this  tree object um there's a bunch of random  like characters and numbers uh this is  the hash for that tree object and then  that hash next to the word parent is  um the hash of the parent so these are  also the file names right so now we know  where to find them  then it says that i'm the author and the  committer and my commit message it's a  test for the pi data talk  and now let's this is actually the tree  object if we look at that  if we go to that file that tree hash  so right now we're looking at my website  actually so my website's a github pages  website so it's all like inner repo  um  so this tree object you can see there's  a bunch of  hashes here and there's things that say  like blob and tree  so blobs are essentially files  so  this blob with the hash at the top is my  dot get ignore  if you  find that  hash and uncompress the file you can see  what's in my data bar  um and then those trees are basically  like  hashes for another tree object and  that's like a folder so it'll list if  you go into that tree object everything  that's in my underscore data folder for  example  um yeah so that that's what's in um  commits and trees so let's talk about  how we branch like how we run  experiments  so  branch information is stored in  dot get slash ref slash heads  so here i've listed out everything  that's in that folder you can see  there's two files  um  so i have a master branch and a test dev  branch and if you just print out what's  in one of those files  it is just a hash  and that hash um is a commit object and  it's the commit or the most recent  commit on that branch  and this is basically it the entire tree  structure isn't stored anywhere in a  file the way that we did basically you  can go  back in the get history by looking at  the parent commit in this commit object  and the parent of that commit and so  like working backwards that way you can  get the entire tree history  so  like we did it lets users create name  branches and they just track the head of  each branch  and one thing i want to point out is  that there is no canonical branch uh the  naming conventions of like master or  main or just naming conventions  um so all branches are the same as far  as git is concerned  so finally how do we do merges  this is actually quite similar to what  we did in our little simple example  so  it's got these tree objects right that  gives a listing of all the files so we  can compare everything in the two trees  and then any new files that are in one  tree or the other we can just accept  those changes in from either branch um  so there's no conflicts there we just  bring those all in and then if the files  have the same name check the hash  and if the hash is matched then like we  did we know  the files are the same so we don't have  any conflict  if the hashes are different  then that means there's been some change  on at least one of the branches so we  need to go into the file to understand  what's going on  and then we follow basically the same  process just at the file level rather  than at the tree level  so we compare everything in the two  files find the most recent common  ancestor of the file and we can do this  by going back in the get history and  looking at the hash of this file at each  time point until you find like matching  hashes essentially  so once we know what that common  ancestor is we can accept any unique  changes from either file so if a line is  changed on branch a but not on branch b  then we accept that change in or if  there's a new line rather  so otherwise if there's then changes on  both  uh then git doesn't know what to do it  has to raise a merge conflict and  basically what that's saying is  there's something going on here that a  user needs to look at and just confirm  what's the actual correct thing to do  um so now we have some basic  understanding of how git does things so  let's talk about how this works in  practice and how we can use it to  understand  how our  workflows work in  in practice  so i've made this goofy grew meme uh  basically what i want to point out here  is get doesn't care about what you have  in your local copy or what i call the  working copy it only cares about what's  in the dot kit folder  um  so the dot get folder  that's all git knows about local changes  that you make don't affect your get  history so you can make changes to all  kinds of files if they're not committed  they don't exist as far as git is  concerned which is great you can make  lots of uh changes and experiment and  try things out  um and it doesn't get stored in your  history uh but this can have some  surprising consequences for people who  aren't used to this paradigm  so again the copy that you're looking at  when you have it in front of you on the  computer that's your working copy  get doesn't know about anything that you  haven't staged or committed  and what that means is if you are on one  branch and then you check out a  different commit or a different branch  it doesn't touch those local changes  so if you are starting to work on a bug  fix but you don't commit those work in  progress changes and then you switch  back to main  those changes will follow you and you'll  have that like partial bug fix in your  working copy which can be a little  surprising to people sometimes  yeah so uncommitted changes follow you  from branch to branch  and if you check out a commit that  conflicts with your working copy  git doesn't know what to do because it  doesn't have the hashes it doesn't have  the tree objects it doesn't try to  resolve it it just says no this this is  on you  you have to like either get rid of these  changes or commit them or do something  because i don't know how to fix this  problem  so the take homes here  before you commit or check out  your code always run a git status  because  if you have forgotten that you like made  some changes  um you might get unexpected results so  it's always good to run a git status  just to make sure everything is as  expected  and then also if you have some work in  progress changes and you want to switch  branches it's a really good idea to  commit them even if they're not done uh  you can mark them as work in progress or  whatever you want but it's really useful  to commit them  so that they don't follow you to the  next branch  so next i want to talk about fetching  and remotes  um so uh  you may have seen this meme before  there's no cloud it's just someone  else's computer so remotes work like  this too and remotes are if this term  isn't familiar this is basically  any other uh copy of the git repository  that's on anyone else's computer so  github is a