 make commits within  github um that's because it is a real  working copy  uh github also gives better user uh  visibility at the user level so you can  see what are all of the repositories  that this person has contributed to  uh things like that and then there's  also like organization level like  concepts and permissioning adds more  security so there's lots of great using  reasons to use github it's very useful  but fundamentally it's another working  copy  uh so just a few final thoughts uh to  wrap up  so  again git  is a really complicated command line  interface and i think that is what  people think of as git  and so it feels very overwhelming and  scary but actually the tool itself is  really simple um it's just like  compressing and hashing everything and  just chucking it into one folder  um  it's like um deceptively simple  so what i want to encourage you to do is  when you run into an error or a conflict  and you like start to panic um don't do  what i did and just like delete your  entire repo just take a step back and  look at it from git's perspective  so take a second to think about like  what is in my docket folder right now  um what's in my working copy and is that  different than what is in the doc get  folder  and then if you're trying to pull and  you get some kind of um error when  you're trying to run a git polar and get  fetch what's in the remote how might  that be different from my dot get how's  that different from my working copy and  how is like the the differences between  these things like how could that be sort  of  creating um the error that i'm seeing  and i also want to make a pitch for  spending a couple hours being frustrated  trying to build git from scratch so  if you try to build your own version  control system it's going to give you a  lot of information about how it works  why it works what's hard about it  um  you know it doesn't need to be something  that you're going to use you can build  it in python you can do whatever  but it's a really interesting and useful  exercise  um so that's pretty much it i want to  leave you with a few resources and i'll  post these slides on my  website  uh so git is simpler than you think is  an article that i referenced really  heavily when i was writing this uh talk  um it really goes into the get internals  at a really understandable level and  it's it's pretty quick read  git internals is an online resource it's  really in the weeds but it's kind of  like a useful reference so i did some of  my like fact checking  uh using that and then i also have to  make a quick pitch for the recurs center  which is sort of like a writer's retreat  for programmers it's all virtual right  now but  um when i went it's like you go in  person into like a big room for a few  months and collaborate with people on  whatever you want and that's where i did  that original exercise to try to build  version control in python from scratch  um so  if you have the opportunity to go to the  record center or program like that it's  a great way to um really learn about  this stuff  um and that is it for me so with that  i'm happy to take any questions  thank you so much eli this is really  exciting stuff my mind is blown i for  sure for sure learned something and el  has a question we have about two minutes  left what is the difference between git  fetch and git pull  uh yeah great question so get fetch  um just does the like emailing into your  dot get folder  thing so it doesn't affect  it it doesn't do a merge into your  branch um it just like pulls those  objects into your dot get  um so i i believe that get pull is  actually just a wrapper around running  get fetch and then get  merge  or something along those lines  awesome hey thank you so much for your  talk and uh  thank you  thanks everyone
 

